## Summary

Hermes Desktop has crossed an important architectural threshold. With the recent multi-source work, it no longer behaves primarily as “one gateway with aliases”; it now has the foundations of a real multi-backend client:

- registered-gateway switching from Sessions;
- explicit `(connectionId, profile)` request routing;
- background sockets retained across foreground changes;
- per-source last-profile state;
- Current-versus-Primary terminology;
- atomic activation with lease/epoch-based publication;
- packaged-build preservation of Nanostores batching;
- adapter-owned messaging session namespaces;
- source-aware Bot thread hydration;
- truthful updater partial-completion/native-binding recovery.

The next architectural problem is no longer route discovery. It is **ownership continuity**.

Desktop increasingly establishes a qualified owner correctly, then some downstream layer treats part of that owner as optional metadata: it drops source/profile/generation, crosses an API boundary, and later reconstructs ownership from endpoint shape, foreground state, a bare resource/session ID, or whichever registration currently looks plausible.

The proposed invariant is:

> **Every Desktop operation and backend-owned object must retain the qualified owner and generation established when authority was obtained. No downstream layer may replace either dimension with foreground state, endpoint inference, bare-ID lookup, or a newly resolved registration.**

This issue is a Desktop-specific architecture interlock. It does not supersede #88680, #90142, #90144, #90145, or the concrete implementation issues below. It connects them into the ownership model Desktop now needs as multi-source behavior becomes normal rather than exceptional.

---

## Why this deserves a Desktop architecture issue

The repository already has the general rules:

- #88680 — preserve Desktop connection × profile route identity end-to-end;
- #90142 — qualified identity must become monotonically more specific, never less;
- #90144 — proof scope must equal mutation scope;
- #90145 — recovery/teardown must be fenced by durable generation identity.

Desktop is where those rules now meet in one product architecture.

A source-qualified route is not sufficient if a later filesystem call uses the foreground gateway.

A qualified route is not sufficient if a stored session ID becomes bare UI identity.

A connection/profile pair is not sufficient if an asynchronous completion from an older activation can still publish into a newer activation.

A correct runtime route is not sufficient if persistence, authorization, outbound delivery, or repair tooling each reconstruct ownership independently.

The Desktop contract therefore needs to make **qualified ownership + generation** one continuous property from creation/resolution through routing, resource access, persistence, UI identity, background work, replay, and teardown.

---

## Architectural model

Desktop should distinguish these concepts explicitly:

```text
Route identity
  = (connectionId, desktopProfile)

Backend namespace
  = (backendId/transportOwner, targetProfile)

Runtime identity
  = (transportOwner, runtimeProfile, activationGeneration)

Durable resource identity
  = (backendOwner, persistenceProfile/store, resourceId, resourceGeneration?)

Authorization identity
  = (owner, authorizationEvidence, authorizationGeneration)

Foreground selection
  = pointer to one current route
```

The exact types can evolve. The important point is that these are **not interchangeable**.

`connectionId` identifies a registered Desktop source.

`desktopProfile` identifies the Desktop-facing route/profile the user selected.

`targetProfile` identifies the backend namespace actually addressed, including alias cases.

`backendId`/transport owner identifies the logical backend independently of foreground selection and, eventually, independently of mutable endpoint text.

`activationGeneration` identifies the specific publication/activation incarnation that authorized follow-on work.

A session/resource/job may then add its own durable generation or lineage where IDs can be reused or ownership can transfer.

---

## The defect class

The common unsafe flow is:

```text
1. Desktop positively resolves qualified owner Q at generation G.
2. A downstream API keeps only a projection of Q.
3. Time/foreground/registry state changes.
4. The downstream layer reconstructs the missing dimensions.
5. The operation executes against Q2/G2 while behaving as though it still represents Q/G.
```

That reconstruction can be internally convincing. Every downstream subsystem may agree on the same reconstructed owner while the operation is nevertheless acting on the wrong source, profile, resource, credential domain, or generation.

This is more dangerous than an obvious routing failure because the system can return success while mutating a valid but unintended backend.

---

## Concrete interlocks

### #89719 / #90048 — shipped route-identity reconstruction residual

#89719 shipped the registered-gateway Sessions switcher and explicit `(connection, profile)` routing foundation.

The important residual is tracked by #90048: after Desktop already has an exact registry `connectionId`, compatibility matching can reduce the descriptor to URL/SSH-host-shaped transport attributes and infer the source again.

That is the canonical anti-pattern:

```text
exact source identity
-> weaker transport coordinates
-> inferred source identity
```

Required rule:

- existing explicit `connectionId` is authoritative;
- do not overwrite it through post-dial inference;
- legacy matching is permitted only for genuinely unqualified descriptors;
- zero matches fail closed;
- multiple matches fail as ambiguity;
- duplicate URL registrations with distinct auth/header/org envelopes remain distinct;
- same-host SSH registrations separated by port/key/path/profile remain distinct.

This issue should treat #90048 as the concrete repair owner, not duplicate it.

### #89916 — resource facade ownership

Filesystem and Git operations must use the same owner as the session, artifact, project, or resource that initiated them.

The foreground route is a UI selection. It is not ownership evidence for background or pinned resources.

Unsafe shape:

```text
session/resource owner = source B / profile worker
foreground route       = source A / profile default
filesystem facade      = path + profile only
result                 = facade re-resolves through source A
```

Required rule:

> Backend-owned resource operations consume the resource's qualified owner, not `currentRoute`.

This applies beyond filesystem/Git to any facade where a resource can outlive or differ from foreground selection.

### #89252 + #89860 — multiplex runtime identity

#89252 should be restacked on #89860 and retain only the complementary architecture that is no longer duplicated.

The remaining model is valuable because one overloaded `profile` field cannot safely represent all of:

- transport owner;
- runtime profile;
- persistence profile/store;
- durable outbound owner;
- authorization evidence;
- repair/migration identity.

The requirement here is not merely to define those dimensions. It is to create a **canonical carrier** and keep it intact through every consumer that can affect routing, keying, storage, batching, replay, callbacks, authorization, or delivery.

If a downstream consumer collapses the carrier back to bare `profile`, endpoint, credential environment, or current runtime state, the architecture has only moved the bug.

### #88764 — provisioning authority

Enumeration and secondary activation are observation/use operations. They do not grant authority to install or provision a new local runtime.

This is the authority-side complement of sticky ownership:

```text
can observe source != can mutate installation
can activate route != can provision runtime
can enumerate profile != can acquire local installation authority
```

A multi-source client must not let discovery or route activation silently escalate into local provisioning because the intended source is absent, unavailable, or not yet materialized locally.

Required rule:

> Observation/use authority never widens into provisioning authority through fallback.

### #88095 and adjacent profile/session work — UI identity

Same-ID sessions across profiles and sources are expected in a real multi-source client.

Therefore:

```text
sessionId
```

cannot remain sufficient identity for:

- foreground session strips;
- panes/tabs;
- stored navigation targets;
- React keys;
- canonical chat pointers;
- unread/activity state;
- hydration caches;
- open/resume actions.

At minimum, UI/session identity needs the owner profile and source. Where ownership can transfer or IDs can be reused, it also needs lineage/generation.

The UI must not become the layer that strips qualified identity immediately before a route-native backend operation.

---

## Generation is the missing temporal dimension

Even `(connectionId, profile)` is not always enough.

Consider:

1. route `(C, P)` activates as generation 41;
2. a background operation captures `(C, P)`;
3. C is removed/recreated, credentials rotate, P is reprovisioned, or activation is superseded;
4. `(C, P)` exists again as generation 42;
5. the old operation resumes.

Name equality does not prove incarnation equality.

The same problem exists for:

- source registrations;
- profile/runtime provisioning;
- activation leases;
- durable resource handles;
- session ownership/lineage;
- authorization evidence;
- background sockets;
- queued outbound delivery;
- repair/recovery operations.

The Desktop rule should therefore be:

> **Qualified identity answers what owns the operation. Generation identity answers which incarnation of that owner authorized it. Both must survive until the mutation boundary.**

For ordering-sensitive state, use monotonic epochs/revisions. For pure incarnation identity, a durable never-reused opaque token may be sufficient.

---

## Foreground state is a selector, not an owner

Desktop now intentionally preserves background sockets/work while foreground selection changes. That makes this distinction non-negotiable.

The following values are useful context but cannot be treated as ownership proof:

- current gateway;
- current profile;
- Primary gateway;
- last-used profile;
- active renderer route;
- currently connected socket;
- endpoint/host that happens to match;
- whichever source is uniquely reachable right now.

Foreground state may resolve **new, genuinely unqualified user intent**.

It must not re-resolve an object or operation that already has an owner.

This should be visible in API design: resource/session/background-work APIs should accept an owner/reference, not implicitly consult foreground state.

---

## Proposed canonical carrier shape

This issue does not require one giant universal type, but Desktop should converge on typed carriers whose projections are deliberate.

Conceptually:

```ts
type DesktopRouteRef = {
  connectionId: string
  desktopProfile: string
}

type RuntimeOwnerRef = {
  route: DesktopRouteRef
  transportOwner: string
  runtimeProfile: string
  activationGeneration: string | number
}

type ResourceOwnerRef = {
  runtimeOwner: RuntimeOwnerRef
  persistenceProfile: string
  storeId?: string
  resourceId: string
  resourceGeneration?: string | number
}
```

Authorization may be attached separately rather than embedded directly:

```ts
type AuthorizationEvidence = {
  owner: DesktopRouteRef | RuntimeOwnerRef
  grantId: string
  generation: string | number
  capabilities: ...
}
```

The precise naming is less important than the invariants:

1. dimensions that determine ownership travel together;
2. generation travels with asynchronous authority;
3. serialization preserves enough identity for replay;
4. resource facades consume resource ownership rather than foreground state;
5. explicit identity cannot be overwritten by inference;
6. compatibility inference terminates at a named boundary and produces a qualified carrier.

---

## API rules

### 1. Resolve once

Genuinely legacy/unqualified input may be resolved at an explicit compatibility boundary.

```text
0 matches -> unresolved
1 match   -> qualified carrier
>1 match  -> ambiguous
```

No first-match or foreground fallback.

### 2. Explicit identity wins

If a descriptor/reference already carries source/profile/backend ownership, that value is authoritative.

Explicit-but-stale/unknown must fail closed. Do not discard it and search for a replacement owner.

### 3. Projections must be visibly lossy

If an API needs only `targetProfile` or only a transport URL, project it locally while retaining the canonical owner in scope.

Do not turn the projection into a new identity carrier.

### 4. Background work captures ownership at creation

Queued work, sockets, session hydration, resource handles, callbacks, and outbound delivery should capture their owner when created.

Later foreground changes must not alter their destination.

### 5. Mutation validates generation

Before an operation publishes, writes, deletes, tears down, provisions, or otherwise commits state, verify that the captured owner generation is still current where the operation requires current ownership.

A stale actor becomes a typed stale/no-op/retry result rather than mutating the successor generation.

### 6. Authority does not widen through fallback

If the requested owner cannot perform an operation, Desktop may report that limitation or explicitly request stronger authority. It must not silently switch to a local/current/default owner whose capabilities are broader.

---

## Resource ownership audit

The following Desktop surfaces should be audited for implicit foreground resolution or insufficient keys:

### Sessions/UI

- session strips and panes;
- stored session IDs/navigation;
- hydration caches;
- canonical Bot chat references;
- unread/activity state;
- open/resume/new-chat actions;
- React keys.

### Resource facades

- filesystem;
- Git;
- projects/workspaces;
- cron/jobs;
- attachments/artifacts;
- settings that belong to a backend/profile;
- plugin-managed durable resources.

### Transport/runtime

- REST clients;
- WebSocket/socket pools;
- SSH connection/session ownership;
- background reconnect;
- activation publication/rollback;
- runtime provisioning and teardown.

### Messaging/multiplexing

- adapter-owned session namespaces;
- batching keys;
- callback/busy/clarify/control state;
- durable outbound queues;
- restored delivery/replay;
- credential-owner/runtime-profile separation.

### Persistence/repair

- route/session/resource serialization;
- migration of legacy unqualified records;
- repair tooling;
- stale registration cleanup;
- source recreation/rebinding.

---

## Adversarial regression matrix

The architecture is not proven by a two-gateway happy path. Tests should deliberately collide the dimensions that old code assumes are unique.

### Source collisions

- [ ] two registered URL connections share the same normalized endpoint but have distinct auth/header/org envelopes;
- [ ] two SSH registrations share host/user but differ by port;
- [ ] same SSH host/user/port but different key/path/profile ownership;
- [ ] explicit `connectionId` remains authoritative under all collisions;
- [ ] legacy unqualified descriptors fail on ambiguity rather than selecting registry order.

### Profile/resource collisions

- [ ] same profile name on local + two remote sources;
- [ ] same session ID on two profiles of one source;
- [ ] same session ID on two different sources;
- [ ] same resource/project/path-shaped identifier on different owners;
- [ ] filesystem/Git calls continue to target the originating owner after foreground switches.

### Temporal/generation collisions

- [ ] rapid route A → B → A with stale B activation completing last;
- [ ] source removed/recreated while an old background operation is in flight;
- [ ] credentials rotate without endpoint changing;
- [ ] profile/runtime reprovisioned under the same names;
- [ ] old resource/session handle resumes after successor generation exists;
- [ ] stale cleanup/rollback cannot mutate the successor generation.

### Authority collisions

- [ ] enumeration of a secondary source cannot provision a local runtime;
- [ ] activation of a remote route cannot fall back to installing/starting a local same-name profile;
- [ ] unavailable remote owner fails explicitly rather than widening authority;
- [ ] authorization evidence for one owner/generation cannot be consumed by another.

### Background/foreground independence

- [ ] foreground source changes while a background socket remains connected;
- [ ] foreground profile changes while queued resource work executes;
- [ ] two Desktop windows foreground different routes while sharing persistence;
- [ ] background event/hydration result cannot attach to a same-ID foreground object owned elsewhere.

---

## Legacy migration contract

Legacy Desktop state will contain records without full owner/generation identity. That state must remain explicitly **unqualified** until it is safely migrated.

Do not interpret missing ownership fields as permission to use:

- current foreground route;
- Primary gateway;
- newest registration;
- endpoint equality;
- only reachable source;
- first registry match;
- same-named profile;
- bare session/resource ID uniqueness observed in the current snapshot.

Those are migration hints, not proof.

Safe options are:

1. exact one-time resolution followed by persistence of the qualified carrier;
2. read-only compatibility until ownership is established;
3. explicit user repair where ambiguity is real;
4. fail-closed behavior for destructive/mutating operations.

Repair tooling from #89252 is particularly important here: ambiguity should be diagnosable and repairable rather than hidden behind fallback.

---

## Relationship to adjacent architecture issues

This issue should link, not replace:

- #88680 — Desktop route preservation and first-class route/resource identity;
- #90142 — repository-wide monotonic qualification rule;
- #90144 — proof scope equals mutation scope;
- #90145 — durable generation fencing;
- #90048 — concrete #89719 shipped residual around descriptor/source reconstruction;
- #89916 — filesystem/Git resource facade ownership;
- #89252 / #89860 — multiplex runtime/transport/storage ownership convergence;
- #88764 — provisioning authority boundary;
- #88095 —
